I think so too.

203
00:10:24,670 --> 00:10:26,550
Okay, everyone, let's get started.

204
00:10:26,550 --> 00:10:27,390
All right.

205
00:10:27,390 --> 00:10:30,350
First, let's talk about the most common question

206
00:10:30,350 --> 00:10:32,190
when it comes to salaries.

207
00:10:32,190 --> 00:10:34,070
Emily, if you're offered a job,

208
00:10:34,070 --> 00:10:36,590
how do you start a salary conversation?

209
00:10:36,590 --> 00:10:37,630
Good question.

210
00:10:37,630 --> 00:10:40,390
A common and polite way to start is by asking,

211
00:10:40,390 --> 00:10:42,030
can we discuss the salary

212
00:10:42,030 --> 00:10:45,310
or I'd like to talk about the compensation package?

213
00:10:45,310 --> 00:10:47,550
That sounds simple and professional.

214
00:10:47,550 --> 00:10:50,910
Can we also say what's the salary for this position?

215
00:10:50,910 --> 00:10:53,870
Yes, you can, but it sounds more direct.

216
00:10:53,870 --> 00:10:58,670
It's okay in some situations, but usually people start with a polite preface like,

217
00:10:58,670 --> 00:11:00,710
I'm very interested in the role.

218
00:11:00,710 --> 00:11:02,270
Could we discuss the salary?

219
00:11:02,270 --> 00:11:03,270
Got it.

220
00:11:03,270 --> 00:11:05,790
So starting politely is always better.

221
00:11:05,790 --> 00:11:10,150
And how do we respond if the employer asks about your salary expectations?

222
00:11:10,150 --> 00:11:12,270
There are a few ways to answer this.

223
00:11:12,270 --> 00:11:17,390
You can give a range, for example, based on my experience I'm looking for $50,000

224
00:11:17,390 --> 00:11:19,870
to $60,000 per year.

225
00:11:19,870 --> 00:11:24,410
Or you can say, I'm flexible, but based on my research, I believe a fair salary for

226
00:11:24,410 --> 00:11:27,230
this role is around $55,000.

227
00:11:27,230 --> 00:11:32,150
Ah, so we give numbers but also sound flexible and professional.

228
00:11:32,150 --> 00:11:33,150
Exactly.

229
00:11:33,150 --> 00:11:34,150
Let's practice that.

230
00:11:34,150 --> 00:11:37,790
Jack, imagine you're asked about your salary expectations.

231
00:11:37,790 --> 00:11:38,790
Okay.

232
00:11:38,790 --> 00:11:44,910
I'd say, based on my experience and skills, I'm looking for a salary between $70,000

233
00:11:44,910 --> 00:11:46,830
and $80,000 per year.

234
00:11:46,830 --> 00:11:51,550
However, I'm open to discussion depending on the benefits and responsibilities.

235
00:11:51,550 --> 00:11:54,990
Beautiful answer. Very professional and confident.

236
00:11:54,990 --> 00:11:59,870
Thank you. And what if the employer offers a number lower than your expectation?

237
00:11:59,870 --> 00:12:04,190
That's when negotiation skills come in. You can politely respond,

238
00:12:04,190 --> 00:12:07,550
thank you for the offer. Based on my research and experience,

239
00:12:07,550 --> 00:12:13,070
I was expecting a salary closer to $75,000. Is there room to adjust?

240
00:12:13,070 --> 00:12:17,070
That's perfect. We're polite, but we also state our value.

241
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:20,070
Exactly. Remember, the key words are,

242
00:12:20,070 --> 00:12:25,570
based on my experience, I was expecting, and is there room to adjust?

243
00:12:25,570 --> 00:12:29,770
Okay, so we avoid saying, I want more money. That sounds rude.

244
00:12:29,770 --> 00:12:32,270
Yes. Always frame it professionally.

245
00:12:32,270 --> 00:12:35,170
So let's give more practical examples.

246
00:12:35,170 --> 00:12:39,270
Imagine someone is negotiating their first job after university.

247
00:12:39,270 --> 00:12:40,770
Sure. You could say,

248
00:12:40,770 --> 00:12:45,730
Thank you for the offer. I'm very excited about the role. I did some research and noticed that

249
00:12:45,730 --> 00:12:52,130
similar positions in this city pay around 50,000 to 55,000 dollars. Would it be possible to consider

250
00:12:52,130 --> 00:12:57,090
something in that range? That sounds very polite and confident. We're showing that we did our

251
00:12:57,090 --> 00:13:03,090
research. Exactly. Salary negotiation is not just about asking for more. It's about showing

252
00:13:03,090 --> 00:13:08,210
that you know your worth and have done your homework. So research is important. How do

253
00:13:08,210 --> 00:13:09,970
How do you research salaries?

254
00:13:09,970 --> 00:13:14,270
There are websites like Glassdoor, LinkedIn, and PayScale.

255
00:13:14,270 --> 00:13:17,930
Also talking to peers in the same industry can give you an idea.

256
00:13:17,930 --> 00:13:22,290
You want to know the average for your role, your experience, and your location.

257
00:13:22,290 --> 00:13:23,650
Okay, that makes sense.

258
00:13:23,650 --> 00:13:27,490
Emily, what about negotiating a raise for a current job?

259
00:13:27,490 --> 00:13:29,490
Ah, this is very common.

260
00:13:29,490 --> 00:13:31,570
First, you need to prepare.

261
00:13:31,570 --> 00:13:36,610
List your accomplishments, extra responsibilities, and any results you delivered.

262
00:13:36,610 --> 00:13:40,210
Then you can ask, I'd like to discuss my current compensation.

263
00:13:40,210 --> 00:13:44,890
Over the past year, I've taken on additional responsibilities and contributed to several

264
00:13:44,890 --> 00:13:46,650
successful projects.

265
00:13:46,650 --> 00:13:50,010
I'd like to explore adjusting my salary accordingly.

266
00:13:50,010 --> 00:13:51,330
That's so professional.

267
00:13:51,330 --> 00:13:54,890
We focus on our achievements, not our personal needs.

268
00:13:54,890 --> 00:13:56,050
Exactly.

269
00:13:56,050 --> 00:13:59,190
Avoid saying, I need more money because of expenses.

270
00:13:59,190 --> 00:14:00,970
That doesn't sound professional.

271
00:14:00,970 --> 00:14:03,090
Focus on your value to the company.

272
00:14:03,090 --> 00:14:04,090
Got it.

273
00:14:04,090 --> 00:14:08,390
If the manager says we don't have budget for a raise right now, then you can ask about

274
00:14:08,390 --> 00:14:09,930
alternative benefits.

275
00:14:09,930 --> 00:14:11,970
For example, I understand.

276
00:14:11,970 --> 00:14:17,450
Could we discuss other options such as additional vacation days, flexible work hours, or a

277
00:14:17,450 --> 00:14:19,490
performance-based bonus?

278
00:14:19,490 --> 00:14:22,530
So negotiation isn't just about the salary.

279
00:14:22,530 --> 00:14:23,530
Correct.

280
00:14:23,530 --> 00:14:25,370
Many benefits can add value.

281
00:14:25,370 --> 00:14:27,210
Let's practice a role play.

282
00:14:27,210 --> 00:14:31,090
Imagine I'm the employee asking for a raise and you're my manager.

283
00:14:31,090 --> 00:14:32,090
Perfect.

284
00:14:32,090 --> 00:14:33,090
Go ahead, Jack.

285
00:14:33,090 --> 00:14:35,470
Emily, thanks for meeting with me today.

286
00:14:35,470 --> 00:14:38,610
I wanted to discuss my current compensation.

287
00:14:38,610 --> 00:14:43,210
Over the past year, I've successfully led two major projects and helped increase our

288
00:14:43,210 --> 00:14:45,870
team's revenue by 20%.

289
00:14:45,870 --> 00:14:49,930
Based on my performance, I'd like to explore adjusting my salary.

290
00:14:49,930 --> 00:14:50,930
Thank you, Jack.

291
00:14:50,930 --> 00:14:52,770
I appreciate your contributions.

292
00:14:52,770 --> 00:14:57,690
Currently, our budget is tight, but we can consider a 5% increase.

293
00:14:57,690 --> 00:14:58,850
Would that work for you?

294
00:14:58,850 --> 00:15:01,690
I really appreciate the offer, Emily.

295
00:15:01,690 --> 00:15:07,270
Based on my research and the impact of my work, I was hoping for a 10% increase.

296
00:15:07,270 --> 00:15:09,950
Is there flexibility to meet halfway?

297
00:15:09,950 --> 00:15:12,830
Perhaps 7% to 8%?

298
00:15:12,830 --> 00:15:14,950
Let me check the budget and get back to you.

299
00:15:14,950 --> 00:15:16,450
I like your approach.

300
00:15:16,450 --> 00:15:19,130
You stayed professional and presented facts.

301
00:15:19,130 --> 00:15:20,130
Thank you, Emily.

302
00:15:20,130 --> 00:15:22,510
I wanted to ensure a fair discussion.

303
00:15:22,510 --> 00:15:24,870
Great example for everyone watching.

304
00:15:24,870 --> 00:15:28,830
Always focus on accomplishments, be specific, and remain professional.

305
00:15:28,830 --> 00:15:32,230
Now, let's talk about tricky language.

306
00:15:32,230 --> 00:15:34,590
Sometimes people get nervous and say things like,

307
00:15:34,590 --> 00:15:37,950
I want a bigger salary or I need more money.

308
00:15:37,950 --> 00:15:38,950
Right.

309
00:15:38,950 --> 00:15:41,990
That sounds casual and can be interpreted as demanding.

310
00:15:41,990 --> 00:15:43,870
Instead, use phrases like,

311
00:15:43,870 --> 00:15:46,870
I'd like to discuss an adjustment to my compensation

312
00:15:46,870 --> 00:15:49,110
or, based on my responsibilities,

313
00:15:49,110 --> 00:15:52,310
I believe my salary should reflect my contribution.

314
00:15:52,310 --> 00:15:53,310
Yes.

315
00:15:53,310 --> 00:15:55,510
Tone and wording are very important.

316
00:15:55,510 --> 00:15:56,830
Absolutely.

317
00:15:56,830 --> 00:16:02,110
Another useful phrase is, I'd like to explore the possibility of a salary adjustment, or

318
00:16:02,110 --> 00:16:05,750
I'd like to review my compensation based on my performance.

319
00:16:05,750 --> 00:16:10,990
Okay, and what about responding if the employer says, we can't meet that number?

320
00:16:10,990 --> 00:16:15,130
You can respond politely, for example, I understand the constraints.

321
00:16:15,130 --> 00:16:20,030
Could we discuss performance goals that would allow a salary increase in the near future?

322
00:16:20,030 --> 00:16:21,030
That's smart.

323
00:16:21,030 --> 00:16:23,750
It shows we're flexible but still proactive.

324
00:16:23,750 --> 00:16:27,310
Now let's discuss negotiating during a job offer.

325
00:16:27,310 --> 00:16:30,430
Sometimes companies give an initial offer lower than expected.

326
00:16:30,430 --> 00:16:33,270
Yes, and many people just accept it.

327
00:16:33,270 --> 00:16:34,430
That's a mistake.

328
00:16:34,430 --> 00:16:35,510
Exactly.

329
00:16:35,510 --> 00:16:37,390
You can say, thank you for the offer.

330
00:16:37,390 --> 00:16:38,950
I'm excited about the role.

331
00:16:38,950 --> 00:16:42,770
I noticed the offer is $55,000 per year.

332
00:16:42,770 --> 00:16:47,810
Based on my experience in market research, I was hoping for $65,000.

333
00:16:47,810 --> 00:16:50,370
Is there any flexibility in the offer?

334
00:16:50,370 --> 00:16:52,450
And if they say yes, great.

335
00:16:52,450 --> 00:16:55,730
If not, you can consider negotiating benefits, right?

336
00:16:55,730 --> 00:16:56,730
Yes.

337
00:16:56,730 --> 00:17:01,970
Benefits like remote work, extra vacation, flexible hours, or training opportunities are

338
00:17:01,970 --> 00:17:04,210
all part of the total package.

339
00:17:04,210 --> 00:17:06,730
Let's practice a job offer negotiation.

340
00:17:06,730 --> 00:17:09,810
I'll be the candidate and you'll be the HR manager.

341
00:17:09,810 --> 00:17:10,810
Okay.

342
00:17:10,810 --> 00:17:11,810
Go ahead, Jack.

343
00:17:11,810 --> 00:17:13,170
Thank you for the offer, Emily.

344
00:17:13,170 --> 00:17:15,330
I'm really excited to join the team.

345
00:17:15,330 --> 00:17:19,410
I noticed the offer is $55,000 per year.

346
00:17:19,410 --> 00:17:24,650
Based on my experience and market research, I was hoping for $65,000.

347
00:17:24,650 --> 00:17:26,970
Is there any flexibility in the offer?

348
00:17:26,970 --> 00:17:29,410
We appreciate your enthusiasm, Jack.

349
00:17:29,410 --> 00:17:35,810
We can offer $60,000 and we provide extra benefits including remote work and health insurance.

350
00:17:35,810 --> 00:17:37,170
Would that work for you?

351
00:17:37,170 --> 00:17:38,290
That sounds great, Emily.

352
00:17:38,290 --> 00:17:40,290
I appreciate your flexibility.

353
00:17:40,290 --> 00:17:44,130
I'm happy to accept $60,000 along with the benefits.

354
00:17:44,130 --> 00:17:45,130
Perfect.

355
00:17:45,130 --> 00:17:47,130
That's a successful negotiation.

356
00:17:47,130 --> 00:17:51,450
See how polite, confident, and clear language helps you get a better outcome?

357
00:17:51,450 --> 00:17:56,290
Now, let's talk about some common mistakes in salary negotiation.

358
00:17:56,290 --> 00:17:58,890
Mistake number one, not doing research.

359
00:17:58,890 --> 00:18:03,370
If you don't know the market rate, you risk asking too much or too little.

360
00:18:03,370 --> 00:18:06,290
Mistake number two, sounding emotional.

361
00:18:06,290 --> 00:18:11,250
Words like, I deserve more or I need money don't sound professional.

362
00:18:11,250 --> 00:18:15,290
Mistake number three, accepting the first offer immediately.

363
00:18:15,290 --> 00:18:18,490
Always pause and consider if you want to negotiate.

364
00:18:18,490 --> 00:18:24,010
Mistake number four, being aggressive, threatening to leave or saying, give me more or I quit

365
00:18:24,010 --> 00:18:25,970
is never professional.

366
00:18:25,970 --> 00:18:29,450
Mistake number five, forgetting to consider benefits.

367
00:18:29,450 --> 00:18:32,330
Total compensation is more than salary.

368
00:18:32,330 --> 00:18:36,070
Mistake number six, not using numbers and facts.

369
00:18:36,070 --> 00:18:40,250
Always refer to your experience, accomplishments, and market rates.

370
00:18:40,250 --> 00:18:42,810
Mistake number seven, not practicing.

371
00:18:42,810 --> 00:18:45,970
play your negotiation before the actual meeting.

372
00:18:45,970 --> 00:18:49,250
Okay, let's do a role play combining everything.

373
00:18:49,250 --> 00:18:51,970
Imagine you are negotiating a promotion.

374
00:18:51,970 --> 00:18:52,970
Perfect.

375
00:18:52,970 --> 00:18:53,970
Go ahead, Jack.

376
00:18:53,970 --> 00:18:54,970
Hi, Emily.

377
00:18:54,970 --> 00:18:58,490
I'd like to discuss my promotion and salary adjustment.

378
00:18:58,490 --> 00:19:04,530
Over the past year, I've managed three major projects, increased sales by 25% and

379
00:19:04,530 --> 00:19:06,450
mentor junior staff.

380
00:19:06,450 --> 00:19:11,050
Considering these contributions, I'd like to explore adjusted nice salary to reflect

381
00:19:11,050 --> 00:19:12,690
my new responsibilities.

382
00:19:12,690 --> 00:19:19,010
Thank you, Jack. We appreciate your hard work. Based on the budget, we can offer a 7% increase.

383
00:19:19,010 --> 00:19:26,090
I appreciate that, Emily. Based on my accomplishments and the market rate, I was hoping for a 12%

384
00:19:26,090 --> 00:19:28,410
increase. Could we meet at 10%?

385
00:19:28,410 --> 00:19:32,690
Let me review this with the finance team. Your approach was professional, focused on

386
00:19:32,690 --> 00:19:34,410
facts and polite.

387
00:19:34,410 --> 00:19:37,810
Thank you, Emily. I wanted to ensure a fair discussion.

388
00:19:37,810 --> 00:19:44,210
example for everyone watching. Always focus on accomplishments, be specific, and remain professional.

389
00:19:44,210 --> 00:19:47,890
Now, before we finish, let's summarize the key points.

390
00:19:47,890 --> 00:19:53,170
First, always start politely. Use phrases like, could we discuss compensation?

391
00:19:53,170 --> 00:19:57,010
Second, know your market value and research salary ranges.

392
00:19:57,010 --> 00:20:00,850
Third, focus on accomplishments, responsibilities, and value.

